    176	The argument: for the `i`-th row of the unfolding, we need a prime in
    177	the half-open interval `(i · W^(d-j), (i+1) · W^(d-j)]`. The interval
    178	length is `W^(d-j)`, the endpoint is `(i+1) · W^(d-j)`. Bertrand's
    179	postulate (`Nat.bertrand` / `Nat.exists_prime_lt_and_le_two_mul`)
    180	provides a prime in `(n, 2n]`, i.e., **interval-length equal to endpoint**.
    181	For our row `i`, interval-length `W^(d-j)` equals endpoint
    182	`(i+1) · W^(d-j)` only when `i = 0`. For `i ≥ 1`, we need primes in
    183	intervals of length `1/(i+1)` of the endpoint — strictly shorter than
    184	Bertrand provides.
